[38;5;252m[1m## Bugs found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three real divergences, all in the Rust:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mmatrix_to_string[0m reallocated instead of overrunning.[22m C sizes the buffer as [38;5;10mh*(11*w) + h + 1[0m but writes [38;5;10msum(digit_len) + h*w + 1[0m bytes, so it overruns whenever values average more than ten characters per cell. The Rust detected this and grew the buffer with [38;5;10mrealloc[0m. That is a fix, not a translation, and it was directly observable: for a 2×1 matrix of [38;5;10m-2147483648[0m, C returned [38;5;10m"-2147483648 -2147483648\n5"[0m (the trailing [38;5;10m5[0m is a byte of the adjacent heap chunk header) where Rust returned the clean string. I rewrote it to perform the same [38;5;10mmalloc(buffer_size)[0m and the same sequence of writes C's [38;5;10mstrcat[0m chain performs, so both now overrun identically — glibc aborts on both for those inputs, which is the ground-truth behavior.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mwrite_to_file[0m captured [38;5;10merrno[0m too early.[22m C evaluates [38;5;10mstrerror(errno)[0m for the message and then reads [38;5;10merrno[0m [3magain[23m for the return value; in the write-failure branch that second read happens after an intervening [38;5;10mfclose(file)[0m. The Rust cached one value and used it for both. Now it re-reads, matching C's evaluation order.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10matoi[0m did not set [38;5;10mERANGE[0m.[22m glibc's [38;5;10matoi[0m is [38;5;10m(int)strtol(...)[0m, which sets [38;5;10merrno = ERANGE[0m on out-of-range input. Since [38;5;10mwrite_to_file[0m returns [38;5;10merrno[0m directly, this was a live leak path. Confirmed the test catches it: with the fix disabled, [38;5;10mdriver(1,1,"99999999999999999999\n",...)[0m leaves [38;5;10mc=34, rust=0[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also switched [38;5;10merrno()[0m from [38;5;10mstd::io::Error::last_os_error()[0m to reading [38;5;10m__errno_location()[0m directly, and dropped the now-unused [38;5;10mrealloc[0m binding.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Harness defect worth knowing about[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mcargo test[0m does not re-emit the cdylib, because no test target links it — integration tests dlopen it instead. My first vacuity check passed against a [38;5;10m.so[0m that was 40 seconds older than the source I had just edited. Any test suite structured this way can silently validate stale code. The harness now compares the [38;5;10m.so[0m mtime against everything under [38;5;10msrc/[0m and [38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m and fails loudly rather than passing vacuously.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Coverage[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Tests are layered bottom-up ([38;5;10mt01[0m → [38;5;10mt09[0m), and every call goes through [38;5;10mlibloading[0m into both [38;5;10m.so[0m files, never into Rust directly, so the [38;5;10m#[no_mangle][0m wrappers are exercised. Beyond return values and matrix contents, I compared exported symbols (all 7, including the non-static [38;5;10mallocate_matrix[0m, with no mangled leakage), stderr byte-for-byte via [38;5;10mdup2[0m redirection, [38;5;10merrno[0m state after each call, and [38;5;10mmatrix.txt[0m contents with [38;5;10mdriver[0m run under a per-implementation cwd. About 3,300 randomized differential cases run from fixed seeds.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two deliberate scoping decisions: [38;5;10mt03[0m sweeps [38;5;10mmatrix_to_string[0m only where C's size estimate provably holds ([38;5;10msum(digit_len) <= 10*w*h + h[0m), including the exact boundary at [38;5;10mw == 1[0m with 11-character values, because beyond it the original has no defined output to compare against. And I did not test null [38;5;10minput[0m to [38;5;10minitialize_matrix_from_string[0m, since C passes it straight to [38;5;10mstrdup[0m and segfaults.[0m[0m
